RayCAD patch to Clipper2 2.0.1 (BSL-1.0; this file is under the same terms).

What: the list of intersections of a sweep is sorted with std::stable_sort instead of std::sort.

Why: intersections at the same point compare equal (IntersectListSort orders by y, then x), and std::sort leaves
equal elements in an order of the standard library's choosing: Microsoft's and GNU's differ. The order changes the
output: the same Union on the same input gave 451 vertices with MSVC and 452 with GCC (the tool-centre area of
spike S5's thin-wall pocket in the adaptive engine, task T-060). A stable
sort keeps the order in which the sweep found the intersections, which is the same everywhere, so every platform
gives the same polygons (RayCAD's determinism rule, PLAN.md section 8). Measured: the same 451 vertices with MSVC
and GCC after the change; Clipper2 already sorts its local minima and horizontal segments with std::stable_sort.

Upstream: not reported yet.

diff --git a/CPP/Clipper2Lib/src/clipper.engine.cpp b/CPP/Clipper2Lib/src/clipper.engine.cpp
--- a/CPP/Clipper2Lib/src/clipper.engine.cpp
+++ b/CPP/Clipper2Lib/src/clipper.engine.cpp
@@ -2453,7 +2453,7 @@ namespace Clipper2Lib {
     //crucial that intersections only occur between adjacent edges.
 
     //First we do a quicksort so intersections proceed in a bottom up order ...
-    std::sort(intersect_nodes_.begin(), intersect_nodes_.end(), IntersectListSort);
+    std::stable_sort(intersect_nodes_.begin(), intersect_nodes_.end(), IntersectListSort);
     //Now as we process these intersections, we must sometimes adjust the order
     //to ensure that intersecting edges are always adjacent ...
 
